.TH "HED-SMART_INDENT" "1" "2026-08-23" "hed v1.21.3" "hed manual"
.SH NAME
hed\-smart_indent \- Carries indentation onto new lines.
When you press  in INSERT mode, the new line starts with the same
leading whitespace (spaces and tabs) as the line you came from.
.PP
Carries indentation onto new lines.
When you press \f[CR]<Enter>\f[R] in INSERT mode, the new line starts
with the same leading whitespace (spaces and tabs) as the line you came
from.
.SH Behavior
.IP \[bu] 2
Mirrors leading whitespace exactly \[em] if the previous line started
with two tabs and four spaces, the new line does too.
.IP \[bu] 2
After \f[CR]{\f[R], \f[CR][\f[R], \f[CR](\f[R] at the end of the
previous line: adds one level of additional indent on top of the carried
indent.
.IP \[bu] 2
After a closing brace at the start of the new line (e.g., you type
\f[CR]}\f[R] on a freshly auto\-indented line): nothing \[em] current
behavior doesn\[cq]t dedent.
Type \f[CR]<BS>\f[R] to clean up.
.SH Notes
The indent unit is a single tab character.
If you use spaces, the carry\-over still works (it copies whatever was
there) but the extra level after \f[CR]{\f[R]/\f[CR][\f[R]/\f[CR](\f[R]
is a tab.
If you want spaces\-only, edit
\f[CR]plugins/smart_indent/smart_indent.c\f[R].
.PP
The plugin hooks \f[CR]HOOK_LINE_INSERT\f[R] (newline) \[em] it does not
run for re\-indenting on \f[CR]<Tab>\f[R] mid\-line, paste, or block
edits.
.SH Disable
Set \f[CR]plugin_load(&plugin_smart_indent, \&...)\f[R] to \f[CR]0\f[R]
in \f[CR]src/config.c\f[R] and \f[CR]:reload\f[R].
